</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email address: </span><a href="mailto:wotao.yin@alibaba-inc.com">wotao.yin@alibaba-inc.com</a>
</span></span></span></span></div>
<div class="ltx_dates">Date: August 24, 2026</div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Implicit models, an emerging model class, compute outputs by iterating a single parameter block to a fixed point. This architecture realizes an infinite-depth, weight-tied network that trains with constant memory, significantly reducing memory needs for the same level of performance compared to explicit models. While it is empirically known that these compact models can often match or even exceed larger explicit networks by allocating more test-time compute, the underlying mechanism remains poorly understood.</p>
    
<p id="abstract1.2" class="ltx_p">We study this gap through a nonparametric analysis of expressive power. We provide a strict mathematical characterization, showing that a simple and regular implicit operator can, through iteration, progressively express more complex mappings. We prove that for a broad class of implicit models, this process lets the model’s expressive power scale with test-time compute, ultimately matching a much richer function class.
The theory is validated across three domains: image reconstruction, scientific computing, and operations research, demonstrating that as test-time iterations increase, the complexity of the learned mapping rises, while the solution quality simultaneously improves and stabilizes.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Many machine-learning tasks can be cast as learning a mapping <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> from input <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> to the desired output <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}_{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}_{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., <math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}_{\ast}={\mathcal{F}}({\bm{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}_{\ast}={\mathcal{F}}({\bm{x}})</annotation></semantics></math>. An emerging alternative is the <span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">implicit models</span> (or named deep equilibrium models, also fixed-point models): train an operator <math id="S1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}</annotation></semantics></math> whose fixed point matches the target, <math id="S1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}_{\ast}={\mathcal{G}}({\bm{y}}_{\ast},{\bm{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}_{\ast}={\mathcal{G}}({\bm{y}}_{\ast},{\bm{x}})</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>. At inference, we repeatedly apply the same learned operator <math id="S1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}</annotation></semantics></math> (weight-tied across all iterations <math id="S1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>):</p>
<table id="S1.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\bm{y}}_{1}={\mathcal{G}}({\bm{y}}_{0},{\bm{x}}),~~{\bm{y}}_{2}={\mathcal{G}}({\bm{y}}_{1},{\bm{x}}),~~{\bm{y}}_{3}={\mathcal{G}}({\bm{y}}_{2},{\bm{x}}),~~\cdots," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.827em">,</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.827em">,</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.549em">,</mo><mo rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}_{1}={\mathcal{G}}({\bm{y}}_{0},{\bm{x}}),~~{\bm{y}}_{2}={\mathcal{G}}({\bm{y}}_{1},{\bm{x}}),~~{\bm{y}}_{3}={\mathcal{G}}({\bm{y}}_{2},{\bm{x}}),~~\cdots,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and expect <math id="S1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}_{t}({\bm{x}})\to{\bm{y}}_{\ast}({\bm{x}})={\mathcal{F}}({\bm{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}_{t}({\bm{x}})\to{\bm{y}}_{\ast}({\bm{x}})={\mathcal{F}}({\bm{x}})</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Rather than producing <math id="S1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}_{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}_{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math> in a single feedforward pass, implicit models reach the target through gradual equilibrium-seeking updates.
Here, “<span id="S1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">test-time compute</span>” refers to the computational budget spent at inference—primarily the number of iterations; increasing this budget increases runtime but not the number of learned parameters.
By tailoring the structure of <math id="S1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}</annotation></semantics></math>, implicit models have shown strong results across many domains (e.g., imaging <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>, scientific computing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">67</a>]</cite>, generative modeling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">73</a>, <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>, LLM reasoning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>, etc.).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Behind these successes, the advantages of implicit models include: <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(i)</span> they realize an infinite-depth, weight-tied network trainable with constant memory, which yields efficient training <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>; <span id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(ii)</span> they allow us to explicitly impose or “implicitly bake in” domain constraints and structure (e.g., physics, geometry, safety), see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib97" title="" class="ltx_ref">97</a>, <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>, <a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">70</a>]</cite>; and, <span id="S1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">most surprisingly,</span> <span id="S1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(iii)</span> <span id="S1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">they can often match or even exceed larger explicit networks by allocating more iterations</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">67</a>, <a href="#bib.bib94" title="" class="ltx_ref">94</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>. Point (i) stems from the weight-tied architecture and avoiding full back-propagation. Point (ii) arises from the inherently implicit nature of many real-world, equation-based constraints. In contrast, the mechanism underlying the surprising effectiveness of (iii) remains less well understood.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We study this through the lens of expressive power—the set of input–output maps a model family can represent. We ask two questions. First, as a baseline:
<span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(Q1)</span> <span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Do implicit models (at least) match the expressive power of explicit ones?</span> Concretely, for a target map <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}:{\bm{x}}\mapsto{\bm{y}}_{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}:{\bm{x}}\mapsto{\bm{y}}_{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math>, does there always exist an implicit operator <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}</annotation></semantics></math> such that the iterates of (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ Implicit Models: Expressive Power Scales with Test-Time Compute" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) satisfy <math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}_{t}({\bm{x}})\to{\mathcal{F}}({\bm{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}_{t}({\bm{x}})\to{\mathcal{F}}({\bm{x}})</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}</annotation></semantics></math>?
If yes, a more insightful question follows:
<span id="S1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(Q2)</span> <span id="S1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Do implicit models offer an expressive advantage?</span> In particular, can a relatively <span id="S1.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">simple</span> implicit operator <math id="S1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}</annotation></semantics></math>, through iteration, represent a <span id="S1.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">complex</span> explicit map <math id="S1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math>? A positive answer to (Q2) would directly explain phenomenon (iii).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To our knowledge, these questions remain largely open, as prior work on the topic is limited. For instance, the universality of implicit models was discussed in specific settings as a secondary result <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">67</a>]</cite>, and a complete theory is missing. Closer to our goal, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib96" title="" class="ltx_ref">96</a>]</cite> focuses on expressive power, partially answering (Q2) by proving a key separation result: some mappings can be realized by an implicit model but not by much larger explicit counterparts. However, a precise characterization of the function class that implicit models can represent remains open. Our work fills this gap from a nonparametric, function-space perspective, revealing a key principle: an implicit model’s expressive power scales with test-time compute. Specifically:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:5mm;">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Expressive boundary.</span> We identify a natural target class (locally Lipschitz mappings)
and prove: simple and well-behaved (which we term “regular”) implicit models, by progressive iterations, can <span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">express any mapping</span> in this class and can <span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">only express mappings</span> in this class.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Emergent expressive power.</span> Our theory yields a new viewpoint on implicit models: the expressive power of a regular implicit operator is not static but <span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">grows with iteration</span> (i.e., scales with test-time compute) and finally matches a much richer function class.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Validation across domains.</span> We validate our theory with case studies in a wide range of applications (e.g., image reconstruction, scientific computing, and operations research). For a representative problem in each domain, we demonstrating that as test-time iterations increase, the empirical complexity of the iterate <math id="S1.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}_{t}({\bm{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}_{t}({\bm{x}})</annotation></semantics></math> rises while solution quality improves and stabilizes.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Main Results</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now return to (Q1): given a target map <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math>, does there exist an implicit operator <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}</annotation></semantics></math> whose fixed-point iteration yields <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}_{t}({\bm{x}})\to{\mathcal{F}}({\bm{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}_{t}({\bm{x}})\to{\mathcal{F}}({\bm{x}})</annotation></semantics></math>? A naive construction answers “yes”: define, for <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\eta&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\eta&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}({\bm{y}},{\bm{x}}):=(1-\eta){\bm{y}}+\eta{\mathcal{F}}({\bm{x}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}({\bm{y}},{\bm{x}}):=(1-\eta){\bm{y}}+\eta{\mathcal{F}}({\bm{x}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Then the fixed-point iteration reduces to <math id="S2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}_{t}=(1-\eta){\bm{y}}_{t-1}+\eta\,{\mathcal{F}}({\bm{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}_{t}=(1-\eta){\bm{y}}_{t-1}+\eta\,{\mathcal{F}}({\bm{x}})</annotation></semantics></math>, hence <math id="S2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}_{t}-{\mathcal{F}}({\bm{x}})=(1-\eta)({\bm{y}}_{t-1}-{\mathcal{F}}({\bm{x}}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}_{t}-{\mathcal{F}}({\bm{x}})=(1-\eta)({\bm{y}}_{t-1}-{\mathcal{F}}({\bm{x}}))</annotation></semantics></math>. As <math id="S2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\eta&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\eta&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, it holds that, for all <math id="S2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}_{t}({\bm{x}})-{\mathcal{F}}({\bm{x}})\to{\bm{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>𝟎</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}_{t}({\bm{x}})-{\mathcal{F}}({\bm{x}})\to{\bm{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, (<a href="#S2.E2" title="In 2. Main Results ‣ Implicit Models: Expressive Power Scales with Test-Time Compute" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) is merely a trivial averaging of <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒚</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}({\bm{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}({\bm{x}})</annotation></semantics></math>; learning such an implicit model is no different from learning <math id="S2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> directly. This prompts the natural follow-up: is there any <em id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">nontrivial</em> implicit representation that is able to indicate the expressive benefits of implicit models?</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">An illustrative example.</span> Let <math id="S2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}(x)=1/x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}(x)=1/x</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[-1,1]\setminus\{0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-1,1]\setminus\{0\}</annotation></semantics></math>. This function is smooth (differentiable to any order) almost everywhere, but blows up near the singular point <math id="S2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=0</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|{\mathcal{F}}(x)|=\left|\frac{1}{x}\right|\to\infty,\quad\left|\frac{\mathrm{d}{\mathcal{F}}}{\mathrm{d}x}\right|=\left|-\frac{1}{x^{2}}\right|\to\infty,\quad\textup{as }x\to 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>x</mi></mfrac><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>as </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|{\mathcal{F}}(x)|=\left|\frac{1}{x}\right|\to\infty,\quad\left|\frac{\mathrm{d}{\mathcal{F}}}{\mathrm{d}x}\right|=\left|-\frac{1}{x^{2}}\right|\to\infty,\quad\textup{as }x\to 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Neural networks approximating <math id="S2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/x</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.p3.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="[-1,-\delta)\cup(\delta,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-1,-\delta)\cup(\delta,1]</annotation></semantics></math> typically demands higher network complexity—i.e., increasing depth/width as <math id="S2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\to 0</annotation></semantics></math> to capture the growing steepness near the singularity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib90" title="" class="ltx_ref">90</a>]</cite>.
If we adopt the naive implicit form (<a href="#S2.E2" title="In 2. Main Results ‣ Implicit Models: Expressive Power Scales with Test-Time Compute" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), <math id="S2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}(y,x)=(1-\eta)y+\eta/x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}(y,x)=(1-\eta)y+\eta/x</annotation></semantics></math>, nothing is gained: the model still inherits the singular behavior <math id="S2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\partial{\mathcal{G}}/\partial x|=\eta/x^{2}\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo rspace="0.055em">/</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\partial{\mathcal{G}}/\partial x|=\eta/x^{2}\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">What would be a nontrivial implicit representation in this setting? Instead of writing <math id="S2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1/x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1/x)</annotation></semantics></math> explicitly, we can regard it as the solution of the equation <math id="S2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="xy-1=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">xy-1=0</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(implicit representation)</span>. Inspired by this, we apply a fixed-point iteration to <math id="S2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="xy-1=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">xy-1=0</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="S2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}(y,x)=y-\eta(xy-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}(y,x)=y-\eta(xy-1)</annotation></semantics></math>. Using the general scheme in (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ Implicit Models: Expressive Power Scales with Test-Time Compute" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), we have <math id="S2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{t}=y_{t-1}-\eta(xy_{t-1}-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{t}=y_{t-1}-\eta(xy_{t-1}-1)</annotation></semantics></math>. Subtracting the true solution gives</p>
<table id="S2.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{t}-\frac{1}{x}=y_{t-1}-\frac{1}{x}-\eta x\left(y_{t-1}-\frac{1}{x}\right)=\left(1-\eta x\right)\left(y_{t-1}-\frac{1}{x}\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>x</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>x</mi></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>x</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>x</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{t}-\frac{1}{x}=y_{t-1}-\frac{1}{x}-\eta x\left(y_{t-1}-\frac{1}{x}\right)=\left(1-\eta x\right)\left(y_{t-1}-\frac{1}{x}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">For any <math id="S2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\eta&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\eta&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math> and any <math id="S2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in(0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in(0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;(1-\eta x)&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;(1-\eta x)&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math> which implies <math id="S2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{t}\to 1/x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{t}\to 1/x</annotation></semantics></math>. (For <math id="S2.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, simply flip the stepsize sign, <math id="S2.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\eta</annotation></semantics></math>.) This implicit formulation is much simpler and more elegant: the operator <math id="S2.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}(y,x)=y-\eta(xy-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}(y,x)=y-\eta(xy-1)</annotation></semantics></math> has <span id="S2.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">no singularity</span> and <span id="S2.p4.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">no blow-up</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The example indicates: intuitively, an implicit representation can realize a complicated map with singularities via a much simpler, smoother update operator <math id="S2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Next, we make it precise: we formally define what we mean by “simple” versus “complex,” and characterize—beyond the <math id="S2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/x</annotation></semantics></math> example—the class of target functions for which an implicit representation admits such a simple form.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Lipschitz continuity)<span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="({\mathbb{X}},\|\cdot\|)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝕏</mi><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{X}},\|\cdot\|)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="({\mathbb{Y}},\|\cdot\|)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝕐</mi><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\mathbb{Y}},\|\cdot\|)</annotation></semantics></math> be normed spaces, and let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{Q}}:{\mathbb{X}}\to{\mathbb{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>𝕏</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>𝕐</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{Q}}:{\mathbb{X}}\to{\mathbb{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We say <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-Lipschitz</em> (globally Lipschitz) on <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕏</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> if there exists <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> such that</p>
<table id="S2.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|{\mathcal{Q}}({\bm{x}}_{1})-{\mathcal{Q}}({\bm{x}}_{2})\|\;\leq\;L\,\|{\bm{x}}_{1}-{\bm{x}}_{2}\|\quad\text{for all }{\bm{x}}_{1},{\bm{x}}_{2}\in{\mathbb{X}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≤</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mtext>for all </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝕏</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|{\mathcal{Q}}({\bm{x}}_{1})-{\mathcal{Q}}({\bm{x}}_{2})\|\;\leq\;L\,\|{\bm{x}}_{1}-{\bm{x}}_{2}\|\quad\text{for all }{\bm{x}}_{1},{\bm{x}}_{2}\in{\mathbb{X}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and the smallest such <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is the <em id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Lipschitz constant</em> (or <em id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Lipschitz modulus</em>), denoted as <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{Lip}({\mathcal{Q}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Lip</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{Lip}({\mathcal{Q}})</annotation></semantics></math>. If the Lipschitz constant <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, we say <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a <span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">contraction</span> with modulus <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> (or <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-<span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">contractive</span>) on <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕏</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{X}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Given <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}\in{\mathbb{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝕏</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}\in{\mathbb{X}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we say <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">locally Lipschitz at <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}</annotation></semantics></math></em> if there exists a neighborhood <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{U}}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> on which <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathbb{U}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝕌</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathbb{U}}</annotation></semantics></math>-Lipschitz continuous for some <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\mathbb{U}}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>𝕌</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\mathbb{U}}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math> is locally Lipschitz at every <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}\in{\mathbb{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝕏</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}\in{\mathbb{X}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we say <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{Q}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{Q}}</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">locally Lipschitz on <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕏</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{X}}</annotation></semantics></math></em>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Intuitively, Lipschitz continuity limits how quickly a function’s value can change. When a function is differentiable, its Lipschitz modulus can be characterized by the norm of its first derivative via the mean-value theorem. For example, <math id="S2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}(x)=1/x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}(x)=1/x</annotation></semantics></math> is locally Lipschitz on <math id="S2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[-1,1]\setminus\{0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-1,1]\setminus\{0\}</annotation></semantics></math> but not globally Lipschitz there, since <math id="S2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathrm{d}{\mathcal{F}}/\mathrm{d}x|=1/x^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathrm{d}{\mathcal{F}}/\mathrm{d}x|=1/x^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is unbounded as <math id="S2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\to 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\to 0</annotation></semantics></math>, causing local Lipschitz constants to blow up near the singularity. In contrast, the implicit update <math id="S2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}(y,x)=y-\eta(xy-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}(y,x)=y-\eta(xy-1)</annotation></semantics></math> has simple partial derivatives <math id="S2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\partial{\mathcal{G}}/\partial x|=|\eta\,y|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo rspace="0.055em">/</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\partial{\mathcal{G}}/\partial x|=|\eta\,y|</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\partial{\mathcal{G}}/\partial y|=|1-\eta x|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">∂</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo rspace="0.055em">/</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\partial{\mathcal{G}}/\partial y|=|1-\eta x|</annotation></semantics></math> without singularity.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Locally Lipschitz mappings form a much richer class than globally Lipschitz ones. Typical examples (locally Lipschitz everywhere in their domains but not globally Lipschitz on the whole set) include: <math id="S2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log x</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tan x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>tan</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tan x</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigl(-\tfrac{\pi}{2},\tfrac{\pi}{2}\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigl(-\tfrac{\pi}{2},\tfrac{\pi}{2}\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mi>x</mi></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{x}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(x)</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}\setminus\{0,-1,-2,\cdots\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}\setminus\{0,-1,-2,\cdots\}</annotation></semantics></math>, etc.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">For this reason, we refer to globally Lipschitz maps as “simple” operators and locally Lipschitz maps (which may exhibit large local slopes near certain inputs) as “complex.” Next, we formally state our main result: identifying a broad family of target functions for which implicit representations provide such simple update operators while expressing complex fixed-point mappings.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 2.2</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{X}}\subset{\mathbb{R}}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕏</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{X}}\subset{\mathbb{R}}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> be bounded and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}:{\mathbb{X}}\to{\mathbb{R}}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>𝕏</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}:{\mathbb{X}}\to{\mathbb{R}}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> be locally Lipschitz on <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕏</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{X}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p9.1" class="ltx_p">We only assume the domain <math id="S2.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕏</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="S2.p9.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">bounded</em>; it need not be compact, closed, or connected.
For instance, <math id="S2.p9.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\mathbb{X}}=[-1,0)\cup(0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕏</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{X}}=[-1,0)\cup(0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> excludes the singular point and permits <math id="S2.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}(x)=1/x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}(x)=1/x</annotation></semantics></math> to blow up at the interior gap <math id="S2.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=0</annotation></semantics></math> while remaining locally Lipschitz on <math id="S2.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕏</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{X}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Another example is <math id="S2.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{X}}=\bigl(-\tfrac{\pi}{2},\tfrac{\pi}{2}\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕏</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{X}}=\bigl(-\tfrac{\pi}{2},\tfrac{\pi}{2}\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}(x)=\tan x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>tan</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}(x)=\tan x</annotation></semantics></math> is locally Lipschitz though it blows up at the boundary points <math id="S2.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm\tfrac{\pi}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mfrac><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm\tfrac{\pi}{2}</annotation></semantics></math> (not in <math id="S2.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕏</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{X}}</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p10.1" class="ltx_p">We now formalize what we mean by “simple” update rules—namely, <em id="S2.p10.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">regular implicit operators</em>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.3</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Regular implicit operator)<span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{X}}\subset{\mathbb{R}}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕏</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{X}}\subset{\mathbb{R}}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> be bounded. An operator <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}:{\mathbb{R}}^{n}\times{\mathbb{X}}\to{\mathbb{R}}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝕏</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}:{\mathbb{R}}^{n}\times{\mathbb{X}}\to{\mathbb{R}}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">regular</em> if: (i) For any <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, the map <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}\mapsto{\mathcal{G}}({\bm{y}},{\bm{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}\mapsto{\mathcal{G}}({\bm{y}},{\bm{x}})</annotation></semantics></math> is globally Lipschitz (w.r.t. <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}</annotation></semantics></math>) on <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕏</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{X}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the Lipschitz constant grows linearly w.r.t. <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|{\bm{y}}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|{\bm{y}}\|</annotation></semantics></math>, and (ii) For each <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}\in{\mathbb{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝕏</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}\in{\mathbb{X}}</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu({\bm{x}})\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu({\bm{x}})\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, the map <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}\mapsto{\mathcal{G}}({\bm{y}},{\bm{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}\mapsto{\mathcal{G}}({\bm{y}},{\bm{x}})</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu({\bm{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu({\bm{x}})</annotation></semantics></math>-contractive on <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu({\bm{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu({\bm{x}})</annotation></semantics></math> is continuous w.r.t. <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Explanation.</span> A regular <math id="S2.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies: (i) Fixing <math id="S2.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒚</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}({\bm{y}},\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}({\bm{y}},\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.p11.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is globally Lipschitz in <math id="S2.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, this makes it a “simple” operator, and (ii) Fixing <math id="S2.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}(\cdot,{\bm{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}(\cdot,{\bm{x}})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.p11.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is contractive in <math id="S2.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}</annotation></semantics></math></span>; by Banach’s theorem, this yields a unique fixed point <math id="S2.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}_{\ast}({\bm{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}_{\ast}({\bm{x}})</annotation></semantics></math> for each <math id="S2.p11.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> and guarantees that iterates of (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ Implicit Models: Expressive Power Scales with Test-Time Compute" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) converge to it: <math id="S2.p11.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}_{t}({\bm{x}})\to{\bm{y}}_{\ast}({\bm{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}_{t}({\bm{x}})\to{\bm{y}}_{\ast}({\bm{x}})</annotation></semantics></math>. <span id="S2.p11.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">An example</span> of such a regular <math id="S2.p11.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the aforementioned <math id="S2.p11.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}(y,x)=y-\eta(xy-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}(y,x)=y-\eta(xy-1)</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.p11.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in(0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in(0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.p11.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\eta&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\eta&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>.
With this definition, we present our main results.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.4</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Sufficiency)<span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Under Assumption <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem2" title="Assumption 2.2. ‣ 2. Main Results ‣ Implicit Models: Expressive Power Scales with Test-Time Compute" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>, for any <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> there exists a <em id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">regular</em> implicit operator <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}:{\mathbb{R}}^{n}\times{\mathbb{X}}\to{\mathbb{R}}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝕏</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}:{\mathbb{R}}^{n}\times{\mathbb{X}}\to{\mathbb{R}}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> whose fixed-point map reproduces <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Fix}\big({\mathcal{G}}(\cdot,{\bm{x}})\big)\;=\;{\mathcal{F}}({\bm{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Fix</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.280em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Fix}\big({\mathcal{G}}(\cdot,{\bm{x}})\big)\;=\;{\mathcal{F}}({\bm{x}})</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}\in{\mathbb{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝕏</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}\in{\mathbb{X}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.5</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Necessity)<span id="S2.Thmtheorem5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{X}}\subset{\mathbb{R}}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕏</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{X}}\subset{\mathbb{R}}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> be bounded and let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}:{\mathbb{R}}^{n}\times{\mathbb{X}}\to{\mathbb{R}}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>𝕏</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}:{\mathbb{R}}^{n}\times{\mathbb{X}}\to{\mathbb{R}}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> be <em id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">regular</em>. Then, for every <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}\in{\mathbb{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>𝕏</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}\in{\mathbb{X}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the map <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}\mapsto{\mathcal{G}}({\bm{y}},{\bm{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}\mapsto{\mathcal{G}}({\bm{y}},{\bm{x}})</annotation></semantics></math> has a unique fixed point <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}_{\ast}({\bm{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}_{\ast}({\bm{x}})</annotation></semantics></math>, and the resulting fixed-point map <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}_{\ast}({\bm{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}_{\ast}({\bm{x}})</annotation></semantics></math> must be locally Lipschitz on <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕏</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{X}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p12.1" class="ltx_p">Proofs are deferred to Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Proofs of main results ‣ Implicit Models: Expressive Power Scales with Test-Time Compute" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>. Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 2.4 (Sufficiency). ‣ 2. Main Results ‣ Implicit Models: Expressive Power Scales with Test-Time Compute" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a> provides an affirmative answer to (Q1) and (Q2) posed in the introduction. It proves that for any locally Lipschitz target <math id="S2.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> on a bounded domain, there exists a <span id="S2.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">regular</span> implicit operator <math id="S2.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}</annotation></semantics></math>, whose iterations converge to the target <math id="S2.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}_{t}({\bm{x}})\to{\mathcal{F}}({\bm{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}_{t}({\bm{x}})\to{\mathcal{F}}({\bm{x}})</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S2.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}</annotation></semantics></math>. This demonstrates that the expressive power of implicit models <span id="S2.p12.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">not only matches</span> that of explicit models <span id="S2.p12.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">but also provides a distinct expressive benefit</span>: <span id="S2.p12.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">a relatively simple (regular) implicit representation can yield a complex fixed-point mapping</span>. Complementarily, Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 2.5 (Necessity). ‣ 2. Main Results ‣ Implicit Models: Expressive Power Scales with Test-Time Compute" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a> shows the boundary is tight: fixed points induced by any regular <math id="S2.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}</annotation></semantics></math> are <em id="S2.p12.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">necessarily</em> locally Lipschitz. Together, the two results give an exact expressivity characterization for regular implicit models.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">What does our theory imply?</span>
Take any locally Lipschitz target <math id="S2.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., the curve in Fig. <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 2. Main Results ‣ Implicit Models: Expressive Power Scales with Test-Time Compute" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). Our results guarantee the existence of a <em id="S2.p13.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">regular</em> implicit operator <math id="S2.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}</annotation></semantics></math> such that the iteration <math id="S2.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}_{t}={\mathcal{G}}({\bm{y}}_{t-1},{\bm{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}_{t}={\mathcal{G}}({\bm{y}}_{t-1},{\bm{x}})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}_{0}={\bm{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟎</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}_{0}={\bm{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> converges: <math id="S2.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}_{t}({\bm{x}})\to{\mathcal{F}}({\bm{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}_{t}({\bm{x}})\to{\mathcal{F}}({\bm{x}})</annotation></semantics></math>. Consider the first iterate:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}_{1}({\bm{x}})={\mathcal{G}}({\bm{0}},{\bm{x}})\quad\implies\quad\textup{Lip}({\bm{y}}_{1})=\sup_{{\bm{x}},{\bm{x}}^{\prime}}\frac{\|{\mathcal{G}}({\bm{0}},{\bm{x}})-{\mathcal{G}}({\bm{0}},{\bm{x}}^{\prime})\|}{\|{\bm{x}}-{\bm{x}}^{\prime}\|}=\textup{Lip}({\mathcal{G}}({\bm{0}},\cdot))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mo stretchy="false">⟹</mo><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mtext>Lip</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></munder><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>Lip</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}_{1}({\bm{x}})={\mathcal{G}}({\bm{0}},{\bm{x}})\quad\implies\quad\textup{Lip}({\bm{y}}_{1})=\sup_{{\bm{x}},{\bm{x}}^{\prime}}\frac{\|{\mathcal{G}}({\bm{0}},{\bm{x}})-{\mathcal{G}}({\bm{0}},{\bm{x}}^{\prime})\|}{\|{\bm{x}}-{\bm{x}}^{\prime}\|}=\textup{Lip}({\mathcal{G}}({\bm{0}},\cdot)).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p13.2" class="ltx_p">Thus, <span id="S2.p13.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">after a single iteration, <math id="S2.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}_{1}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}_{1}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is exactly as smooth as <math id="S2.p13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}({\bm{0}},\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}({\bm{0}},\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. By Definition <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem3" title="Definition 2.3 (Regular implicit operator). ‣ 2. Main Results ‣ Implicit Models: Expressive Power Scales with Test-Time Compute" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>, <math id="S2.p13.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}({\bm{0}},\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}({\bm{0}},\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is globally Lipschitz in <math id="S2.p13.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> with a <em id="S2.p13.2.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">uniform</em> constant, hence the map <math id="S2.p13.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}_{1}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}_{1}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> inherits the same property.
With more iterations, <math id="S2.p13.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> approaches <math id="S2.p13.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math>, so the Lipschitz constant of <math id="S2.p13.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}_{t}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}_{t}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> approaches that of <math id="S2.p13.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lim_{t\to\infty}\frac{\|{\bm{y}}_{t}({\bm{x}})-{\bm{y}}_{t}({\bm{x}}^{\prime})\|}{\|{\bm{x}}-{\bm{x}}^{\prime}\|}=\frac{\|{\mathcal{F}}({\bm{x}})-{\mathcal{F}}({\bm{x}}^{\prime})\|}{\|{\bm{x}}-{\bm{x}}^{\prime}\|}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">lim</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></munder><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{t\to\infty}\frac{\|{\bm{y}}_{t}({\bm{x}})-{\bm{y}}_{t}({\bm{x}}^{\prime})\|}{\|{\bm{x}}-{\bm{x}}^{\prime}\|}=\frac{\|{\mathcal{F}}({\bm{x}})-{\mathcal{F}}({\bm{x}}^{\prime})\|}{\|{\bm{x}}-{\bm{x}}^{\prime}\|}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p13.3" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S2.p13.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> has singularities (where local slopes are large or even unbounded), then the effective Lipschitz constants of <math id="S2.p13.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}_{t}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}_{t}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> necessarily grow with <math id="S2.p13.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> to match that complexity—illustrated in Fig. <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 2. Main Results ‣ Implicit Models: Expressive Power Scales with Test-Time Compute" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. In short: <span id="S2.p13.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">a regular operator <math id="S2.p13.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}</annotation></semantics></math> gains expressive power through iteration (“test-time compute”)</span>, ultimately matching a richer function class that can include singularities and unbounded Lipschitz behavior.</p>
</div>
