</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2. </span>Case Study 2: Scientific Computing</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Navier–Stokes (NS) equations are foundational to computational fluid dynamics. We focus on the 2D steady-state incompressible case on a periodic domain <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega:=[0,2\pi]^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>:=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega:=[0,2\pi]^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(7)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(u\cdot\nabla)u+\nabla p=\nu\Delta u+f,\quad\nabla\cdot u=0\quad\textup{on $\Omega$}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mo>∇</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>+</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>ν</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>f</mi><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mo>∇</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mrow><mtext>on </mtext><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(u\cdot\nabla)u+\nabla p=\nu\Delta u+f,\quad\nabla\cdot u=0\quad\textup{on $\Omega$}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u:\Omega\to{\mathbb{R}}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u:\Omega\to{\mathbb{R}}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is the velocity field, <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p:\Omega\to{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p:\Omega\to{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the pressure, <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> is the viscosity, <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:\Omega\to{\mathbb{R}}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\Omega\to{\mathbb{R}}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is the external force. Solving NS equations refers to determining <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> solves (<a href="#S3.E7" title="In 3.2. Case Study 2: Scientific Computing ‣ 3. Case Studies ‣ Implicit Models: Expressive Power Scales with Test-Time Compute" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>) given <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>.
Although global existence/smoothness of the solution given general forcings is famously open, classical results guarantee well-posedness under suitable conditions on <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.6</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib91" title="" class="ltx_ref">91</a>]</cite>)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There exists a constant <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> depending only on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega</annotation></semantics></math> such that, if <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|f\|_{L^{2}(\Omega)}\leq c\,\nu^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|f\|_{L^{2}(\Omega)}\leq c\,\nu^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, then </span>(<a href="#S3.E7" title="In 3.2. Case Study 2: Scientific Computing ‣ 3. Case Studies ‣ Implicit Models: Expressive Power Scales with Test-Time Compute" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> admits a unique solution <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{\ast}(f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{\ast}(f)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{H}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℍ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{H}}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the space of admissible forcings<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note"><span id="footnote1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">1</span></span>
                  
                  
                  
                <span id="footnote1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">Details regarding the function spaces are provided in Appendix </span><a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Proofs regarding NS Equations ‣ Implicit Models: Expressive Power Scales with Test-Time Compute" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_upright"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a><span id="footnote1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">.</span></span></span></span>, and set <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{B}}_{\nu}:=\{f\in{\mathbb{H}}:\ \|f\|_{L^{2}(\Omega)}\leq c\nu^{2}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔹</mi><mi>ν</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℍ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.778em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ν</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{B}}_{\nu}:=\{f\in{\mathbb{H}}:\ \|f\|_{L^{2}(\Omega)}\leq c\nu^{2}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then there exists a subset <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{H}}_{\nu}\subset{\mathbb{B}}_{\nu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ℍ</mi><mi>ν</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>𝔹</mi><mi>ν</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{H}}_{\nu}\subset{\mathbb{B}}_{\nu}</annotation></semantics></math> that is dense in <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{B}}_{\nu}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔹</mi><mi>ν</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{B}}_{\nu}</annotation></semantics></math>, on which the solution map <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\mapsto u_{\ast}(f)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\mapsto u_{\ast}(f)</annotation></semantics></math> is locally Lipschitz.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Vorticity form.</span> Let <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega:=\nabla\times u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo>∇</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega:=\nabla\times u</annotation></semantics></math> (and hence <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{\ast}:=\nabla\times u_{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo>∇</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{\ast}:=\nabla\times u_{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math>). Under periodic boundary and zero-mean conditions, one can recover the velocity <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> from vorticity <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> by solving a Poisson equation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">64</a>]</cite>. We hence focus on the solution map in vorticity: <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\mapsto\omega_{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\mapsto\omega_{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">While Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem6" title="Theorem 3.6 ([]). ‣ 3.2. Case Study 2: Scientific Computing ‣ 3. Case Studies ‣ Implicit Models: Expressive Power Scales with Test-Time Compute" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.6</span></a> gives a local Lipschitz result in function spaces, our expressivity results (Section <a href="#S2" title="2. Main Results ‣ Implicit Models: Expressive Power Scales with Test-Time Compute" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) are stated for finite-dimensional spaces. To bridge this gap, we discretize the NS equations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Discretization.</span>
Partition <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> cells <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{h}:=\{C_{i}\}_{i=1}^{N_{h}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{h}:=\{C_{i}\}_{i=1}^{N_{h}}</annotation></semantics></math> and define the cell–average restriction <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{R}}_{h}(f)|_{C}:=\frac{1}{|C|}\int_{C}f(\xi)\mathrm{d}\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>C</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi>C</mi></msub><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{R}}_{h}(f)|_{C}:=\frac{1}{|C|}\int_{C}f(\xi)\mathrm{d}\xi</annotation></semantics></math> (similarly for <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>). We work with the discrete forcings and vorticities:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}:={\mathcal{R}}_{h}(f)\in{\mathbb{R}}^{N_{h}\times 2},\quad{\bm{y}}:={\mathcal{R}}_{h}(\omega)\in{\mathbb{R}}^{N_{h}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}:={\mathcal{R}}_{h}(f)\in{\mathbb{R}}^{N_{h}\times 2},\quad{\bm{y}}:={\mathcal{R}}_{h}(\omega)\in{\mathbb{R}}^{N_{h}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">and aim to learn <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}\mapsto{\bm{y}}_{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}\mapsto{\bm{y}}_{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}_{\ast}:={\mathcal{R}}_{h}(\omega_{\ast})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}_{\ast}:={\mathcal{R}}_{h}(\omega_{\ast})</annotation></semantics></math> is the discrete solution in vorticity form. Back to the continuum setting, let the lifting operator <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{E}}_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{E}}_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> be the piecewise–constant reconstruction <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{E}}_{h}({\bm{x}}):=\sum_{C\in\Omega_{h}}x_{C}{\bm{1}}_{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mn>𝟏</mn><mi>C</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{E}}_{h}({\bm{x}}):=\sum_{C\in\Omega_{h}}x_{C}{\bm{1}}_{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{P}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the orthogonal projection onto divergence–free, zero–mean fields.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 3.7</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The mapping <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}_{2}:{\bm{x}}\mapsto{\bm{y}}_{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}_{2}:{\bm{x}}\mapsto{\bm{y}}_{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math> is locally Lipschitz continuous on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{X}}_{\nu,h}:=\{{\bm{x}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{N_{h}\times 2}:{\mathcal{P}}({\mathcal{E}}_{h}({\bm{x}}))\in{\mathbb{H}}_{\nu}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝕏</mi><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℍ</mi><mi>ν</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{X}}_{\nu,h}:=\{{\bm{x}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{N_{h}\times 2}:{\mathcal{P}}({\mathcal{E}}_{h}({\bm{x}}))\in{\mathbb{H}}_{\nu}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Consequently, there exists a regular implicit operator <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}({\bm{y}},{\bm{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}({\bm{y}},{\bm{x}})</annotation></semantics></math> satisfying <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{Fix}({\mathcal{G}}(\cdot,{\bm{x}}))={\mathcal{F}}_{2}({\bm{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>Fix</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{Fix}({\mathcal{G}}(\cdot,{\bm{x}}))={\mathcal{F}}_{2}({\bm{x}})</annotation></semantics></math> on any bounded subset of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{X}}_{\nu,h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕏</mi><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{X}}_{\nu,h}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The corollary instantiates our expressivity theory for steady-state NS, guaranteeing the existence of a <em id="S3.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">regular</em> implicit model <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}</annotation></semantics></math>. As in the image–reconstruction case, we now (i) choose a problem-specific parameterization of <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}</annotation></semantics></math> and (ii) verify our theory numerically on this architecture.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem-tailored parameterization.</span>
We use <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">67</a>]</cite> as our code base. In particular,</p>
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(8)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{z}}_{\ast}={\mathcal{G}}_{\Theta}\!\big({\bm{z}}_{\ast},\ {\mathcal{Q}}_{\Phi}({\bm{x}})\big),\qquad{\bm{y}}_{\ast}={\mathcal{Q}}_{\Psi}({\bm{z}}_{\ast})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{z}}_{\ast}={\mathcal{G}}_{\Theta}\!\big({\bm{z}}_{\ast},\ {\mathcal{Q}}_{\Phi}({\bm{x}})\big),\qquad{\bm{y}}_{\ast}={\mathcal{Q}}_{\Psi}({\bm{z}}_{\ast}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p6.2" class="ltx_p">The core <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}_{\Theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}_{\Theta}</annotation></semantics></math> is implemented as a Fourier Neural Operator (FNO) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>]</cite>, and both the encoder <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{Q}}_{\Phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{Q}}_{\Phi}</annotation></semantics></math> and decoder <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{Q}}_{\Psi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{Q}}_{\Psi}</annotation></semantics></math> use pointwise MLPs<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
              
              
              
            Introducing additional encoder and decoder is common in practice. Compared to the vanilla formulation <math id="footnote2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}_{\ast}={\mathcal{G}}({\bm{y}}_{\ast},{\bm{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}_{\ast}={\mathcal{G}}({\bm{y}}_{\ast},{\bm{x}})</annotation></semantics></math>, it does not change our expressivity results in Section <a href="#S2" title="2. Main Results ‣ Implicit Models: Expressive Power Scales with Test-Time Compute" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. Details appear in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B A variant architecture ‣ Implicit Models: Expressive Power Scales with Test-Time Compute" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.</span></span></span>. Details appear in Appendix <a href="#A7" title="Appendix G Experiment Details regarding Scientific Computing ‣ Implicit Models: Expressive Power Scales with Test-Time Compute" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">There is a growing literature on AI for scientific computing (e.g., <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib78" title="" class="ltx_ref">78</a>, <a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>, <a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>, <a href="#bib.bib77" title="" class="ltx_ref">77</a>, <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>, <a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>]</cite>; see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>, <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>]</cite> for surveys). We adopt (<a href="#S3.E8" title="In 3.2. Case Study 2: Scientific Computing ‣ 3. Case Studies ‣ Implicit Models: Expressive Power Scales with Test-Time Compute" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>) because it learns solution <em id="S3.SS2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">operators</em> rather than per-instance solutions, yielding discretization/mesh-resolution invariance, fast global mixing via Fourier layers, and strong parameter efficiency.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Experiments.</span> We use the dataset of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">67</a>]</cite> with viscosity <math id="S3.SS2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu=0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu=0.01</annotation></semantics></math>, which provides <math id="S3.SS2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4500" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4500</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4500</annotation></semantics></math> training pairs and <math id="S3.SS2.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="500" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>500</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">500</annotation></semantics></math> test pairs <math id="S3.SS2.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\bm{x}},{\bm{y}}^{\ast})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\bm{x}},{\bm{y}}^{\ast})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS2.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the discretized force and <math id="S3.SS2.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}^{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}^{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math> is the corresponding vorticity; we denote these sets by <math id="S3.SS2.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{D}}_{\textup{pde,train}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mtext>pde,train</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{D}}_{\textup{pde,train}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{D}}_{\textup{pde,test}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mtext>pde,test</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{D}}_{\textup{pde,test}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Details are given in Appendix <a href="#A7" title="Appendix G Experiment Details regarding Scientific Computing ‣ Implicit Models: Expressive Power Scales with Test-Time Compute" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p9.1" class="ltx_p">We test iteration-wise behavior for <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>50</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">50</annotation></semantics></math> steps starting from <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{z}}_{0}={\bm{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟎</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{z}}_{0}={\bm{0}}</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{z}}_{t+1}={\mathcal{G}}_{\Theta}\big({\bm{z}}_{t},{\mathcal{Q}}_{\Phi}({\bm{x}})\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{z}}_{t+1}={\mathcal{G}}_{\Theta}\big({\bm{z}}_{t},{\mathcal{Q}}_{\Phi}({\bm{x}})\big)</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq t\leq 49" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>49</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq t\leq 49</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}_{t}({\bm{x}})={\mathcal{Q}}_{\Psi}({\bm{z}}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}_{t}({\bm{x}})={\mathcal{Q}}_{\Psi}({\bm{z}}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Analogous to the inverse-problem study, we augment the test set with perturbations.
For each <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\bm{x}}_{i},{\bm{y}}_{i}^{\ast})\in{\mathbb{D}}_{\textup{pde,test}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mtext>pde,test</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\bm{x}}_{i},{\bm{y}}_{i}^{\ast})\in{\mathbb{D}}_{\textup{pde,test}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we construct <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>15</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">15</annotation></semantics></math> perturbed vorticities <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{{\bm{y}}^{\ast}_{i,j}\}_{j=1}^{15}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒚</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>15</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{{\bm{y}}^{\ast}_{i,j}\}_{j=1}^{15}</annotation></semantics></math>; we then compute compatible forces <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{{\bm{x}}_{i,j}\}_{j=1}^{15}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>15</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{{\bm{x}}_{i,j}\}_{j=1}^{15}</annotation></semantics></math> by evaluating the NS operator (see Appendix <a href="#A7" title="Appendix G Experiment Details regarding Scientific Computing ‣ Implicit Models: Expressive Power Scales with Test-Time Compute" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G</span></a> for details).
The perturbed test set is <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\mathbb{D}}^{\prime}_{\textup{pde,test}}=\{({\bm{x}}_{i,j},{\bm{y}}^{\ast}_{i,j}):1\leq i\leq 500,\ 1\leq j\leq 15\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝔻</mi><mtext>pde,test</mtext><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒚</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>500</mn><mo>,</mo><mn> 1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>15</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{D}}^{\prime}_{\textup{pde,test}}=\{({\bm{x}}_{i,j},{\bm{y}}^{\ast}_{i,j}):1\leq i\leq 500,\ 1\leq j\leq 15\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Across iterations we report an empirical Lipschitz estimate <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and relative reconstruction error <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{t}:=\max_{1\leq i\leq 500}\max_{1\leq j\leq 15}\frac{\|{\bm{y}}_{t}({\bm{x}}_{i})-{\bm{y}}_{t}({\bm{x}}_{i,j})\|}{\|{\bm{x}}_{i}-{\bm{x}}_{i,j}\|},\quad\textup{and}\quad E_{t}(i,j):=\frac{\|{\bm{y}}_{t}({\bm{x}}_{i,j})-{\bm{y}}^{\ast}_{i,j}\|}{\|{\bm{y}}^{\ast}_{i,j}\|+\epsilon}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>500</mn></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow></munder></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒚</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒚</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{t}:=\max_{1\leq i\leq 500}\max_{1\leq j\leq 15}\frac{\|{\bm{y}}_{t}({\bm{x}}_{i})-{\bm{y}}_{t}({\bm{x}}_{i,j})\|}{\|{\bm{x}}_{i}-{\bm{x}}_{i,j}\|},\quad\textup{and}\quad E_{t}(i,j):=\frac{\|{\bm{y}}_{t}({\bm{x}}_{i,j})-{\bm{y}}^{\ast}_{i,j}\|}{\|{\bm{y}}^{\ast}_{i,j}\|+\epsilon},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p9.2" class="ltx_p">for <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq 500,0\leq j\leq 15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>500</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq 500,0\leq j\leq 15</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j=0</annotation></semantics></math> means the original (unperturbed) sample, <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}_{i,0}:={\bm{x}}_{i},{\bm{y}}^{\ast}_{i,0}:={\bm{y}}^{\ast}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝒚</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}_{i,0}:={\bm{x}}_{i},{\bm{y}}^{\ast}_{i,0}:={\bm{y}}^{\ast}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> evaluates accuracy on both <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{D}}_{\textup{pde,test}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mtext>pde,test</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{D}}_{\textup{pde,test}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p9.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{D}}^{\prime}_{\textup{pde,test}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝔻</mi><mtext>pde,test</mtext><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{D}}^{\prime}_{\textup{pde,test}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p10.1" class="ltx_p">The results in Figure <a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 3.2. Case Study 2: Scientific Computing ‣ 3. Case Studies ‣ Implicit Models: Expressive Power Scales with Test-Time Compute" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> align with our theory.
At <math id="S3.SS2.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=1</annotation></semantics></math>, the mapping <math id="S3.SS2.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}_{1}({\bm{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}_{1}({\bm{x}})</annotation></semantics></math> reflects a single application of <math id="S3.SS2.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}_{\Theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}_{\Theta}</annotation></semantics></math> and exhibits low Lipschitz constant: <math id="S3.SS2.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{1}=23.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>23.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{1}=23.1</annotation></semantics></math>.
As iterations proceed toward the fixed point, the complexity grows markedly: <math id="S3.SS2.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> increases to <math id="S3.SS2.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx 367" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mn>367</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx 367</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S3.SS2.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>50</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=50</annotation></semantics></math> (Figure <a href="#S3.F4.sf1" title="In Figure 4 ‣ 3.2. Case Study 2: Scientific Computing ‣ 3. Case Studies ‣ Implicit Models: Expressive Power Scales with Test-Time Compute" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4(a)</span></a>).
Meanwhile, the relative error <math id="S3.SS2.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> decreases monotonically and stabilizes at <math id="S3.SS2.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.078\pm 0.028" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.078</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.028</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.078\pm 0.028</annotation></semantics></math> (Figure <a href="#S3.F4.sf2" title="In Figure 4 ‣ 3.2. Case Study 2: Scientific Computing ‣ 3. Case Studies ‣ Implicit Models: Expressive Power Scales with Test-Time Compute" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4(b)</span></a>), indicating convergence to a good approximation of <math id="S3.SS2.p10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}_{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}_{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, <span id="S3.SS2.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">the learned operator <math id="S3.SS2.p10.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}_{\Theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}_{\Theta}</annotation></semantics></math> is simple (Lipschitz in <math id="S3.SS2.p10.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}</annotation></semantics></math>), while additional test-time iterations let <math id="S3.SS2.p10.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> realize progressively more complex mappings.</span>
In addition, a comparison with an explicit baseline (vanilla FNO) in Figure <a href="#S3.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 3.2. Case Study 2: Scientific Computing ‣ 3. Case Studies ‣ Implicit Models: Expressive Power Scales with Test-Time Compute" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> shows the implicit model produces more accurate solutions, both visually and quantitatively.
Additional experiments showing a small implicit model outperforming larger explicit ones appear in Appendix <a href="#A7" title="Appendix G Experiment Details regarding Scientific Computing ‣ Implicit Models: Expressive Power Scales with Test-Time Compute" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F4" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S3.F4.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.03638v1/lip_plot_ns.svg" id="S3.F4.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/215;" width="476" height="215"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F4.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S3.F4.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Empirical Lipschitz <math id="S3.F4.sf1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> of the <math id="S3.F4.sf1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-step map <math id="S3.F4.sf1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}_{t}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}_{t}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> vs. iteration. <math id="S3.F4.sf1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> starts small at <math id="S3.F4.sf1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t{=}1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t{=}1</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S3.F4.sf1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="23.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>23.1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">23.1</annotation></semantics></math>) and grows substantially, reaching <math id="S3.F4.sf1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim 367" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∼</mo><mn>367</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim 367</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S3.F4.sf1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t{=}50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>50</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t{=}50</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S3.F4.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.03638v1/err_plot_ns.svg" id="S3.F4.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/215;" width="476" height="215"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F4.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S3.F4.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Relative error <math id="S3.F4.sf2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> (mean <math id="S3.F4.sf2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math> std over original and perturbed inputs) vs. iteration. <math id="S3.F4.sf2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> decreases to <math id="S3.F4.sf2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.078\pm 0.028" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.078</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.028</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.078\pm 0.028</annotation></semantics></math>—<math id="S3.F4.sf2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> converges towards <math id="S3.F4.sf2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}_{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}_{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>. </span><span id="S3.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Validation on the steady Navier–Stokes task. Iterating a simple operator <math id="S3.F4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}_{\Theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}_{\Theta}</annotation></semantics></math> yields a complex fixed-point mapping: Lipschitz constant (a) increases, while error (b) decreases.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
