</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3. </span>Case Study 3: Operations Research</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Linear programming (LP) is fundamental to operations research, of which a general form is given by</p>
<table id="S3.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(9)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min_{{\bm{y}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{n}}{\bm{c}}^{\top}{\bm{y}},\quad\textup{s.t. }{\bm{A}}{\bm{y}}\circ{\bm{b}},~~{\bm{l}}\leq{\bm{y}}\leq{\bm{u}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>s.t. </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑨</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>𝒃</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.827em">,</mo><mrow><mi>𝒍</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>𝒖</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{{\bm{y}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{n}}{\bm{c}}^{\top}{\bm{y}},\quad\textup{s.t. }{\bm{A}}{\bm{y}}\circ{\bm{b}},~~{\bm{l}}\leq{\bm{y}}\leq{\bm{u}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{A}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{m\times n},{\bm{b}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{m},{\bm{c}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{n},{\bm{l}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{n},{\bm{u}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑨</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝒃</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝒄</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝒍</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝒖</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{A}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{m\times n},{\bm{b}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{m},{\bm{c}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{n},{\bm{l}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{n},{\bm{u}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\circ\in\{=,\leq\}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">=</mo><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">≤</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\circ\in\{=,\leq\}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes component-wise relations, i.e., each <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\circ_{i}\in\{=,\leq\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∘</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em">∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">=</mo><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">≤</mo><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\circ_{i}\in\{=,\leq\}</annotation></semantics></math> specifies whether <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\bm{A}}{\bm{y}})_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝑨</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\bm{A}}{\bm{y}})_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> equals or is bounded above by <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\bm{x}}:=({\bm{A}},{\bm{b}},{\bm{c}},\circ,{\bm{l}},{\bm{u}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑨</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒃</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒄</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∘</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒍</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒖</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}:=({\bm{A}},{\bm{b}},{\bm{c}},\circ,{\bm{l}},{\bm{u}})</annotation></semantics></math> as the input that describes the LP in (<a href="#S3.E9" title="In 3.3. Case Study 3: Operations Research ‣ 3. Case Studies ‣ Implicit Models: Expressive Power Scales with Test-Time Compute" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>). To define the solution mapping <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> that maps <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> to the solution of LP, we require feasibility and boundedness (which ensure an optimal solution <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>). Accordingly, let</p>
<table id="S3.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\mathbb{X}}:=\{({\bm{A}},{\bm{b}},{\bm{c}},\circ,{\bm{l}},{\bm{u}}):\textup{The resulting LP is feasible and bounded}\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝕏</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑨</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒃</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒄</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∘</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒍</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒖</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mtext>The resulting LP is feasible and bounded</mtext><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{X}}:=\{({\bm{A}},{\bm{b}},{\bm{c}},\circ,{\bm{l}},{\bm{u}}):\textup{The resulting LP is feasible and bounded}\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Within <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕏</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{X}}</annotation></semantics></math>, there are some LPs where the solution mapping is not single-valued or not continuous. By excluding these LPs, it forms a subset <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{X}}_{\mathrm{sub}}\subset{\mathbb{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝕏</mi><mi>sub</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mi>𝕏</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{X}}_{\mathrm{sub}}\subset{\mathbb{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> on which <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> is single-valued and locally Lipschitz. The strict definition of <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{X}}_{\mathrm{sub}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕏</mi><mi>sub</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{X}}_{\mathrm{sub}}</annotation></semantics></math> and the proof of Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem8" title="Theorem 3.8. ‣ 3.3. Case Study 3: Operations Research ‣ 3. Case Studies ‣ Implicit Models: Expressive Power Scales with Test-Time Compute" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.8</span></a> are provided in Appendix <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Proofs regarding linear programming ‣ Implicit Models: Expressive Power Scales with Test-Time Compute" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.8</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There is a subset <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{X}}_{\mathrm{sub}}\subset{\mathbb{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝕏</mi><mi>sub</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mi>𝕏</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{X}}_{\mathrm{sub}}\subset{\mathbb{X}}</annotation></semantics></math> that is dense in <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{X}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕏</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{X}}</annotation></semantics></math>, on which each LP admits a unique solution <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}_{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}_{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the solution map <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{F}}_{3}:{\bm{x}}\mapsto{\bm{y}}_{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{F}}_{3}:{\bm{x}}\mapsto{\bm{y}}_{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math> is locally Lipschitz continuous on <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{X}}_{\mathrm{sub}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕏</mi><mi>sub</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{X}}_{\mathrm{sub}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 3.9</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There must be a regular implicit model <math id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}({\bm{y}},{\bm{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}({\bm{y}},{\bm{x}})</annotation></semantics></math> that satisfies <math id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textup{Fix}({\mathcal{G}}(\cdot,{\bm{x}}))={\mathcal{F}}_{3}({\bm{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>Fix</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textup{Fix}({\mathcal{G}}(\cdot,{\bm{x}}))={\mathcal{F}}_{3}({\bm{x}})</annotation></semantics></math> on any bounded subsets of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{X}}_{\mathrm{sub}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕏</mi><mi>sub</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{X}}_{\mathrm{sub}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Corollary <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem9" title="Corollary 3.9. ‣ 3.3. Case Study 3: Operations Research ‣ 3. Case Studies ‣ Implicit Models: Expressive Power Scales with Test-Time Compute" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.9</span></a> follows immediately from Theorems <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 2.4 (Sufficiency). ‣ 2. Main Results ‣ Implicit Models: Expressive Power Scales with Test-Time Compute" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a> and <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem8" title="Theorem 3.8. ‣ 3.3. Case Study 3: Operations Research ‣ 3. Case Studies ‣ Implicit Models: Expressive Power Scales with Test-Time Compute" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.8</span></a>. It indicates the existence of implicit models with desired properties that solves LP. As in the previous case studies, we now (i) choose a problem-specific parameterization of <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}</annotation></semantics></math> and (ii) verify the theory numerically on this architecture.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Implicit GNN parameterization.</span> We model the implicit operator <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}</annotation></semantics></math> for LP with a <span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">graph neural network (GNN)</span>. First, express an LP instance <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\bm{x}}=({\bm{A}},{\bm{b}},{\bm{c}},\circ,{\bm{l}},{\bm{u}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑨</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒃</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒄</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∘</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒍</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒖</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}=({\bm{A}},{\bm{b}},{\bm{c}},\circ,{\bm{l}},{\bm{u}})</annotation></semantics></math> as a bipartite graph (Figure <a href="#S3.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 3.3. Case Study 3: Operations Research ‣ 3. Case Studies ‣ Implicit Models: Expressive Power Scales with Test-Time Compute" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>). We create <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> variable nodes <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{V_{j}\}_{j=1}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{V_{j}\}_{j=1}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> constraint nodes <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{W_{i}\}_{i=1}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{W_{i}\}_{i=1}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math>. Node features collect the data of the LP: each <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> stores <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(c_{j},l_{j},u_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(c_{j},l_{j},u_{j})</annotation></semantics></math>; each <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> stores <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(b_{i},\circ_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∘</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(b_{i},\circ_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>. We connect <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{ij}\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{ij}\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, and place <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> on that edge as its feature.
Given this representation, an (explicit) GNN can map the LP to a solution, i.e., <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}_{\ast}=\textup{GNN}({\bm{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>GNN</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}_{\ast}=\textup{GNN}({\bm{x}})</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the graph-encoded LP.
This approach was proposed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite> in the context of mixed-integer linear programs, and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> subsequently showed that (explicit) GNNs offer a universal framework for representing LPs. Built on this, we propose an <em id="S3.SS3.p3.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">implicit</em> GNN:</p>
<table id="S3.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(10)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{z}}_{\ast}={\mathcal{G}}_{\Theta}({\bm{z}}_{\ast},{\mathcal{Q}}_{\Phi}({\bm{x}})),\quad{\bm{y}}_{\ast}={\mathcal{Q}}_{\Psi}({\bm{z}}_{\ast})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{z}}_{\ast}={\mathcal{G}}_{\Theta}({\bm{z}}_{\ast},{\mathcal{Q}}_{\Phi}({\bm{x}})),\quad{\bm{y}}_{\ast}={\mathcal{Q}}_{\Psi}({\bm{z}}_{\ast})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}_{\Theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}_{\Theta}</annotation></semantics></math> is the core GNN, <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{Q}}_{\Phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{Q}}_{\Phi}</annotation></semantics></math> encodes instance-specific (static) features from <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{Q}}_{\Psi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{Q}}_{\Psi}</annotation></semantics></math> decodes per-variable states to the solution. Both <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{Q}}_{\Phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{Q}}_{\Phi}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{Q}}_{\Psi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{Q}}_{\Psi}</annotation></semantics></math> are small MLPs shared across all nodes.
At inference, we repeatedly call <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}_{\Theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}_{\Theta}</annotation></semantics></math> with initialization <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{z}}_{0}={\bm{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟎</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{z}}_{0}={\bm{0}}</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{z}}_{t}={\mathcal{G}}_{\Theta}({\bm{z}}_{t-1},{\mathcal{Q}}_{\Phi}({\bm{x}}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{z}}_{t}={\mathcal{G}}_{\Theta}({\bm{z}}_{t-1},{\mathcal{Q}}_{\Phi}({\bm{x}}))</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=1,2,\cdots,T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=1,2,\cdots,T</annotation></semantics></math>, and finally output <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}_{t}={\mathcal{Q}}_{\Psi}({\bm{z}}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}_{t}={\mathcal{Q}}_{\Psi}({\bm{z}}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>. Relative to prior work, our only modification is to attach to each variable node an additional <em id="S3.SS3.p3.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">dynamic</em> state <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{j}^{(t)}\in{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{j}^{(t)}\in{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Details appear in Appendix <a href="#A8" title="Appendix H Experiment Details regarding LP ‣ Implicit Models: Expressive Power Scales with Test-Time Compute" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">H</span></a>.</p>
</div>
