C 23.11 102.36 25.59 99.88 25.59 96.83 L 25.59 -4.31 C 25.59 -7.36 23.11 -9.84 20.06 -9.84 L -211 -9.84 C -214.06 -9.84 -216.54 -7.36 -216.54 -4.31 Z M 25.59 102.36"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#969696;" stroke="#969696" stroke-dasharray="3.0pt,3.0pt" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-width="1.0pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 32.28 -9.84 M 32.28 -4.31 L 32.28 96.83 C 32.28 99.88 34.76 102.36 37.82 102.36 L 161.79 102.36 C 164.84 102.36 167.32 99.88 167.32 96.83 L 167.32 -4.31 C 167.32 -7.36 164.84 -9.84 161.79 -9.84 L 37.82 -9.84 C 34.76 -9.84 32.28 -7.36 32.28 -4.31 Z M 167.32 102.36"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#969696;" stroke="#969696" stroke-dasharray="3.0pt,3.0pt" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-width="1.0pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 177.17 -9.84 M 177.17 -4.31 L 177.17 96.83 C 177.17 99.88 179.64 102.36 182.7 102.36 L 368.48 102.36 C 371.54 102.36 374.02 99.88 374.02 96.83 L 374.02 -4.31 C 374.02 -7.36 371.54 -9.84 368.48 -9.84 L 182.7 -9.84 C 179.64 -9.84 177.17 -7.36 177.17 -4.31 Z M 374.02 102.36"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#969696;--ltx-fill-color:#969696;" fill="#969696" stroke="#969696" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -147.61 -32.64)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:7.54em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.3" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="104.28"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S3.F6.pic1.16" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#969696;">A linear program</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#969696;--ltx-fill-color:#969696;" fill="#969696" stroke="#969696" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 35.87 -32.64)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:9.35em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.3" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="129.44"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S3.F6.pic1.17" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#969696;">Graph representation</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#969696;--ltx-fill-color:#969696;" fill="#969696" stroke="#969696" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 190.21 -32.64)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:13.65em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.3" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="188.86"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S3.F6.pic1.18" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#969696;">Implicit GNN iteratively called</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</span></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F6.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 6</span>. </span><span id="S3.F6.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">The graph representation of LP and implicit GNN applied on this graph</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">There is a rapidly growing literature on implicit GNNs with diverse applications and theories <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>, <a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">72</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">58</a>, <a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">69</a>, <a href="#bib.bib103" title="" class="ltx_ref">103</a>, <a href="#bib.bib100" title="" class="ltx_ref">100</a>]</cite>. Our LP case study is complementary to this line of work: rather than adopting a particular implicit-GNN architecture, we start from a standard explicit GNN for LP and convert it into a fixed-point formulation tailored to linear programs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In addition, recent work on ML for LP has progressed quickly (e.g., <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>, <a href="#bib.bib84" title="" class="ltx_ref">84</a>, <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">55</a>, <a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>, <a href="#bib.bib76" title="" class="ltx_ref">76</a>, <a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>, <a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">62</a>]</cite>); for broader context, see overviews on Learning to Optimize <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> and AI for Operations Research <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>. Our goal here is not to outperform all state-of-the-arts; rather, we aim to establish a foundational point: converting a standard LP-GNN into an implicit form yields the benefits predicted by our theory—implicit models can be simple at the update level yet expressive at the fixed-point map, and their performance improves predictably with test-time iteration.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Experiments.</span> We sample LP instances <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\bm{x}}=({\bm{A}},{\bm{b}},{\bm{c}},\circ,{\bm{l}},{\bm{u}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑨</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒃</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒄</mi><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∘</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒍</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒖</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}=({\bm{A}},{\bm{b}},{\bm{c}},\circ,{\bm{l}},{\bm{u}})</annotation></semantics></math>, solve it to obtain an optimal solution <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}_{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}_{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math>, and form <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2{,}500" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2,500</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2{,}500</annotation></semantics></math> training pairs and <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1{,}000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1,000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1{,}000</annotation></semantics></math> test pairs like <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\bm{x}},{\bm{y}}_{\ast})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\bm{x}},{\bm{y}}_{\ast})</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{D}}_{\textup{LP,train}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mtext>LP,train</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{D}}_{\textup{LP,train}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{D}}_{\textup{LP,test}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mtext>LP,test</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{D}}_{\textup{LP,test}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We also create five perturbed test sets <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{{\mathbb{D}}^{(j)}_{\textup{LP,test}}\}_{j=1}^{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝔻</mi><mtext>LP,test</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>5</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{{\mathbb{D}}^{(j)}_{\textup{LP,test}}\}_{j=1}^{5}</annotation></semantics></math> by altering exactly one block among
(<math id="S3.SS3.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{A}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑨</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{A}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{b}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒃</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{b}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{c}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒄</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{c}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{l}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒍</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{l}}</annotation></semantics></math>, or <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{u}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒖</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{u}}</annotation></semantics></math>).
For each <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\bm{x}}_{i},{\bm{y}}_{i}^{\ast})\in{\mathbb{D}}_{\textup{LP,test}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝔻</mi><mtext>LP,test</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\bm{x}}_{i},{\bm{y}}_{i}^{\ast})\in{\mathbb{D}}_{\textup{LP,test}}</annotation></semantics></math> and each perturbation type <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>, we form a perturbed instance <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math>, solve it to obtain <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}^{\ast}_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝒚</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}^{\ast}_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math>, and collect
<math id="S3.SS3.p6.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{D}}^{(j)}_{\textup{LP,test}}=\{({\bm{x}}_{i,j},{\bm{y}}^{\ast}_{i,j})\}_{i=1}^{1000}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝔻</mi><mtext>LP,test</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒚</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>1000</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{D}}^{(j)}_{\textup{LP,test}}=\{({\bm{x}}_{i,j},{\bm{y}}^{\ast}_{i,j})\}_{i=1}^{1000}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Details in Appendix <a href="#A8" title="Appendix H Experiment Details regarding LP ‣ Implicit Models: Expressive Power Scales with Test-Time Compute" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">H</span></a>.
We report:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{t}(j):=\max_{1\leq i\leq 1000}\frac{\|{\bm{y}}_{t}({\bm{x}}_{i})-{\bm{y}}_{t}({\bm{x}}_{i,j})\|}{\|{\bm{x}}_{i}-{\bm{x}}_{i,j}\|},\quad\textup{and}\quad E_{t}(i,j):=\frac{\|{\bm{y}}_{t}({\bm{x}}_{i,j})-{\bm{y}}^{\ast}_{i,j}\|}{\|{\bm{y}}^{\ast}_{i,j}\|+\epsilon}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1000</mn></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒚</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒚</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{t}(j):=\max_{1\leq i\leq 1000}\frac{\|{\bm{y}}_{t}({\bm{x}}_{i})-{\bm{y}}_{t}({\bm{x}}_{i,j})\|}{\|{\bm{x}}_{i}-{\bm{x}}_{i,j}\|},\quad\textup{and}\quad E_{t}(i,j):=\frac{\|{\bm{y}}_{t}({\bm{x}}_{i,j})-{\bm{y}}^{\ast}_{i,j}\|}{\|{\bm{y}}^{\ast}_{i,j}\|+\epsilon},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p6.2" class="ltx_p">for <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq 1000,0\leq j\leq 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1000</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq 1000,0\leq j\leq 5</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j=0</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the unperturbed pair <math id="S3.SS3.p6.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\bm{x}}_{i,0},{\bm{y}}^{\ast}_{i,0}):=({\bm{x}}_{i},{\bm{y}}^{\ast}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒚</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\bm{x}}_{i,0},{\bm{y}}^{\ast}_{i,0}):=({\bm{x}}_{i},{\bm{y}}^{\ast}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Results support our theory.
<span id="S3.SS3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">(i)</span> Figure <a href="#S3.F7.sf1" title="In Figure 7 ‣ 3.3. Case Study 3: Operations Research ‣ 3. Case Studies ‣ Implicit Models: Expressive Power Scales with Test-Time Compute" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7(a)</span></a> plots the five curves <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{t}(j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{t}(j)</annotation></semantics></math> (one for each perturbation type). At <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=1</annotation></semantics></math>, a single application of (<a href="#S3.E10" title="In 3.3. Case Study 3: Operations Research ‣ 3. Case Studies ‣ Implicit Models: Expressive Power Scales with Test-Time Compute" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>) yields relatively small empirical Lipschitz constants for <span id="S3.SS3.p7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">all</span> perturbation modes. As iterations proceed toward the fixed point, Lipschitz constants grow markedly.
<span id="S3.SS3.p7.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">(ii)</span> Figure <a href="#S3.F7.sf2" title="In Figure 7 ‣ 3.3. Case Study 3: Operations Research ‣ 3. Case Studies ‣ Implicit Models: Expressive Power Scales with Test-Time Compute" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7(b)</span></a> reports the mean<math id="S3.SS3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math>std of <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{t}(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{t}(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> decreases and stabilizes at <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.146" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.146</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.146</annotation></semantics></math>, indicating that the growth of <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> reflects the higher intrinsic complexity of the solution mapping <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}_{\ast}({\bm{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}_{\ast}({\bm{x}})</annotation></semantics></math> rather than divergence or instability.
<span id="S3.SS3.p7.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">(iii)</span> Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3.3. Case Study 3: Operations Research ‣ 3. Case Studies ‣ Implicit Models: Expressive Power Scales with Test-Time Compute" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> contrasts implicit and explicit GNNs. At matched embedding sizes, implicit GNNs match or beat explicit ones—most clearly at small/mid sizes (4/8/16). In addition, a smaller implicit model can outperform a larger explicit model on training error. For example, implicit–4 vs. explicit–8 (0.203 vs. 0.233) and implicit–8 vs. explicit–16 (0.162 vs. 0.183). This supports our theory that iterating a simple implicit operator can yield strong expressivity.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F7" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S3.F7.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.03638v1/lip_plot_lp.svg" id="S3.F7.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/215;" width="476" height="215"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F7.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S3.F7.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Empirical Lipschitz <math id="S3.F7.sf1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> of the <math id="S3.F7.sf1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-step map <math id="S3.F7.sf1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}_{t}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}_{t}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> vs. iteration. <math id="S3.F7.sf1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> starts small at <math id="S3.F7.sf1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t{=}1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t{=}1</annotation></semantics></math> and grows substantially by <math id="S3.F7.sf1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t{=}8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t{=}8</annotation></semantics></math> for all perturbation modes.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S3.F7.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.03638v1/err_plot_lp.svg" id="S3.F7.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/215;" width="476" height="215"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F7.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S3.F7.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Relative error <math id="S3.F7.sf2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> (mean <math id="S3.F7.sf2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math> std over original and perturbed inputs) vs. iteration. <math id="S3.F7.sf2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> decreases to <math id="S3.F7.sf2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.146\pm 0.091" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.146</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.091</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.146\pm 0.091</annotation></semantics></math>—<math id="S3.F7.sf2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> converges towards <math id="S3.F7.sf2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}_{\ast}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}_{\ast}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F7.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 7</span>. </span><span id="S3.F7.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Numerical validation on the linear-program task.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS3.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Discussion on generalization.</span>
While generalization is not our main focus, a trend in Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3.3. Case Study 3: Operations Research ‣ 3. Case Studies ‣ Implicit Models: Expressive Power Scales with Test-Time Compute" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> is informative: explicit GNNs improve as width increases from 4 to 8 but then <span id="S3.SS3.p8.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">overfit</span> (test error significantly rises at 16/32), whereas implicit GNNs improve from 4 to 8 to 16 and only tick up slightly at 32. We attribute this to: (i) LP constraints <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{A}}{\bm{y}}\circ{\bm{b}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑨</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒚</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>𝒃</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{A}}{\bm{y}}\circ{\bm{b}}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S3.E9" title="In 3.3. Case Study 3: Operations Research ‣ 3. Case Studies ‣ Implicit Models: Expressive Power Scales with Test-Time Compute" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>) are specified implicitly rather than as an explicit set; implicit models align naturally with such a structure, and (ii) while fixed-point maps <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}_{\ast}({\bm{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}_{\ast}({\bm{x}})</annotation></semantics></math> can be sensitive to inputs <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}</annotation></semantics></math>, the implicit formulation allows us realize them via a simpler, smaller operator <math id="S3.SS3.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{G}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which “implicitly” regularizes training and support good generalization in practice.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S3.T1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 1</span>. </span><span id="S3.T1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Comparison between explicit GNNs and implicit GNNs on the LP task.</span></figcaption>
<table id="S3.T1.5" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S3.T1.5.1" class="ltx_tr">
